First Author: Zadeh
Title: Predictive analytics for de novo malignancies after lung transplantation
Source number: 55  ￼

Very short summary: This study developed machine-learning models to predict de novo malignancy after lung transplantation using U.S. STAR/UNOS data. Five key findings were: (1) the final analytic cohort included 30,917 lung-transplant recipients without prior malignancy, of whom 25% developed post-transplant malignancy; (2) the initial lung-transplant dataset comprised 66,590 cases, which was reduced after excluding prior malignancy and loss to follow-up; (3) several ML methods were compared, with gradient boosting performing best; (4) the best model achieved AUROC 0.746; and (5) important predictors included recipient DR52 antigen, BMI, CMV status, and total serum albumin. Figures 1–2 and Tables 1 and the methods/results descriptions were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

