	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The study developed several supervised models, including decision trees, logistic regression, neural networks, gradient boosting, random forest, and SVM, and reported gradient boosting as best-performing. Inputs came from merged STAR sources including waitlist, transplant, donor, and follow-up datasets, which are tabular recipient/donor variables. The data were split into 70% training and 30% validation/testing. Variable review by lung-transplant experts excluded irrelevant, redundant, and highly missing variables, so Manual/Clinical is the best fit for feature selection. The paper explicitly describes setting/tuning key parameters for gradient boosting and random forest.  ￼

