	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study used a large U.S. national transplant registry dataset and included 30,917 lung-transplant recipients after explicit exclusions, which reduces major center-level selection bias.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors included recipient, donor, and follow-up variables, but the retrieved text indicates a mix of pre-, during-, and post-transplant measures, which may complicate interpretation depending on intended clinical use.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study specifically identified individuals “diagnosed with malignant cancers during their respective follow-up periods,” and excluded those with prior malignancy, giving a clear definition of de novo malignancy.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include a large registry-derived cohort, multiple model comparisons, class-imbalance handling, and a held-out validation set. Uncertainty remains because the retrieved text does not show full reporting of calibration or all test metrics, and some predictor timing may affect transportability.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The paper directly addresses machine-learning prediction of a clinically important post-lung-transplant outcome and is relevant to thoracic-transplant AI, even though the endpoint is malignancy rather than survival or rejection.  ￼

